    "explanation": "A Service Control Policy attached to the OU establishes the maximum permission ceiling for every account and role beneath it. A Deny statement keyed on the aws:RequestedRegion condition (with a NotAction for global services) blocks resource creation outside the allowed Regions regardless of what IAM policies member-account admins write, because an SCP deny overrides any account-level IAM allow. This is centrally managed at the OU, so new accounts inherit it automatically — minimal ongoing effort.\n\nYou would not attach per-role IAM policies (B) because they must be created and maintained in every account, and a member-account admin could simply change or remove them. An AWS Config rule (C) only detects noncompliant resources after they exist; it is detective, not preventive, so it does not stop the creation. A permissions boundary (D) caps an individual principal's permissions but must be applied per principal and does not blanket the whole organization.\n\nFor the exam, also know the SCP fundamentals: SCPs filter or deny but never grant, so a principal still needs an IAM allow; an explicit deny always wins; and the management account is exempt from SCPs. Distinguish SCP (org-wide guardrail) from permissions boundary (per-principal cap) from IAM policy (the actual grant).",
