    "explanation": "An Auto Scaling group spanning two Availability Zones behind an Application Load Balancer is the canonical highly available, self-healing web tier. The ALB distributes traffic only to healthy targets across both AZs, and Auto Scaling replaces any instance that fails a health check and keeps the fleet at the desired capacity, so the tier survives both instance failures and an entire AZ outage.\n\nYou would not rely on a single larger instance with a reboot alarm because it remains a single point of failure in one AZ; a reboot does not help if the AZ or the host fails. Two instances in the same AZ removes the instance-level SPOF but still fails when that one AZ goes down. A single instance in each AZ with Route 53 simple routing has no health checking or automatic replacement, and simple routing returns records at random rather than steering away from a failed endpoint.\n\nFor the exam, also know the difference between EC2 health checks (does the instance respond) and ELB health checks (does the application respond on the target). Auto Scaling can use either, and enabling ELB health checks lets it replace instances that are running but no longer serving traffic.",
