    "explanation": "An ELB routes requests only to registered targets that are currently passing its health checks, and by sitting between clients and the fleet it decouples the traffic source from the instances doing the work, so instances can be added, removed, or replaced without the caller noticing. Those are the two accurate statements.\n\nThe load balancer did not prevent instances from launching; instance creation is governed by EC2 capacity and the account's On-Demand quota, not by the ELB. The ELB also does not provision instances at all; Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling launches and terminates instances, and the ELB merely load-balances across whatever healthy targets exist. Finally, a single ELB spans multiple Availability Zones by design (that is central to how it provides high availability), so it is not limited to one AZ.\n\nFor the exam, also know the clean division of labor: Auto Scaling manages how many instances exist and replaces unhealthy ones, while ELB decides how traffic is spread across the healthy ones. Confusing which component creates capacity is a classic distractor.",
    "difficulty": 2,
