    "topicId": "d2-ha-ft",
    "type": "single",
    "stem": "An application uses a single-AZ Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL instance. The team's ONLY goal is to survive the failure of the database's Availability Zone with automatic failover and no loss of committed transactions. Which change BEST meets this goal?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Enable Multi-AZ, which keeps a synchronous standby in another AZ and fails over automatically"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Add two read replicas in other AZs to serve traffic during an outage"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Increase the instance size and turn on storage autoscaling"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Schedule hourly automated snapshots copied to a second Region"
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "RDS Multi-AZ provisions a synchronous standby replica in a different Availability Zone. Because replication is synchronous, committed transactions are on the standby before they are acknowledged, and if the primary AZ fails RDS automatically fails over to the standby by flipping the database endpoint. That directly delivers the requested AZ-fault survival with no committed-data loss.\n\nYou would not use read replicas for this goal because they replicate asynchronously (risking a small amount of data loss) and are designed to scale read traffic, not to provide automatic failover; promoting a replica is a manual, higher-latency operation. Increasing instance size and enabling storage autoscaling improves performance and capacity but does nothing for AZ resilience. Hourly cross-Region snapshots protect against disasters but involve data loss up to an hour and a manual restore, which is not automatic failover.\n\nFor the exam, keep Multi-AZ versus read replica straight: Multi-AZ is synchronous and for availability/failover; read replicas are asynchronous and for read scaling (though they can be promoted for DR). Aurora blends both, keeping six copies across three AZs with fast failover to a replica.",
