    "explanation": "The exam principle is to choose the *cheapest* strategy that still meets the required RTO/RPO. A few-minutes RTO with near-zero data loss rules out the slower, cheaper options but does not require the most expensive one, so warm standby is the best fit: a scaled-down but always-running, fully functional stack in the DR Region can absorb traffic and scale up within minutes.\n\nBackup & restore (A) recovers in hours - too slow. Pilot light (B) has its core servers switched off and must provision and scale them on failover, giving an RTO in tens of minutes, which exceeds a few-minutes target. Multi-site active-active (D) would meet the RTO but runs full production in multiple Regions simultaneously, costing significantly more than warm standby - so it fails the \"lowest cost\" constraint even though it is technically capable.\n\nFor the exam, also know that tightening RTO/RPO always trades cost for speed; do not over-provision to active-active when a warm standby already satisfies the objective at lower cost.",
