    "explanation": "Enabling S3 Versioning (A) and adding a Lifecycle rule that expires noncurrent versions after 90 days (B) is the correct pair. Versioning preserves prior versions so an accidental overwrite or delete can be rolled back (a delete simply adds a delete marker while older versions remain), and the Lifecycle rule automatically purges those noncurrent versions after 90 days so retained history does not accumulate unbounded cost.\n\nYou would not choose One Zone-IA (C): changing storage class addresses per-GB cost and AZ redundancy, not recoverability of overwritten or deleted objects, and it actually reduces resilience. Object Lock in compliance mode (D) would make objects immutable and would conflict with the goal of automatically expiring old versions, so it is the wrong tool for recoverable, self-cleaning history.\n\nCross-Region Replication (E) copies objects to another Region for DR but does nothing to let you undo deletes in the source bucket or expire old versions on its own.\n\nFor the exam, also know that Lifecycle rules distinguish current from noncurrent versions: use NoncurrentVersionExpiration to trim version history and Expiration for current objects, and pair versioning with lifecycle to balance protection against cost.",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "tags": [
      "S3",
      "versioning",
      "lifecycle",
      "data-protection"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon S3 > User Guide > Using versioning in S3 buckets",
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon S3 > User Guide > Managing the lifecycle of objects"
    ],
    "inShort": "Versioning enables undoing deletes/overwrites; a Lifecycle rule expiring noncurrent versions after 90 days controls cost.",
    "trap": "Object Lock compliance mode would block the automatic expiry of old versions, conflicting with the cost-cleanup goal."
