    "explanation": "Taking EBS snapshots and copying them to the second Region, automated with Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager or AWS Backup, is correct. EBS snapshots are incremental point-in-time backups that AWS stores durably in S3 and are scoped to a single Region; copying a snapshot to us-east-1 gives you a Region-independent restore point, and DLM or AWS Backup schedules the snapshots and cross-Region copies automatically.\n\nYou would not use EBS Multi-Attach: it lets multiple instances in the SAME Availability Zone share one io1/io2 volume and provides no cross-Region or even cross-AZ capability, so it cannot serve as a DR mechanism.\n\nAmazon EFS in us-west-2 keeps the data inside the failed Region and does not by itself replicate block-volume data to another Region. A cross-Region RAID 1 mirror is not a supported or workable EBS configuration; EBS volumes are AZ-scoped block devices and cannot be mirrored across Regions.\n\nFor the exam, also know the snapshot-versus-AMI distinction: a snapshot backs up a single EBS volume, while an AMI captures the full instance (root volume plus configuration). Both are Region-scoped and can be copied cross-Region, and AWS Backup can centrally manage cross-Region and cross-account copies.",
