    "correct": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "explanation": "Statements A and B are true. S3 Replication requires S3 Versioning to be enabled on BOTH the source and destination buckets, because replication tracks and copies specific object versions. And replication only acts on objects created AFTER the rule is enabled; to copy objects that already existed when replication was configured, you run S3 Batch Replication to backfill them.\n\nStatement C is false: replication is asynchronous, so a successful PUT to bucket A returns before the object has been copied to bucket B; there is no synchronous, cross-Region write guarantee.\n\nStatement D is false: replication supports cross-account destinations, so bucket B can be owned by a different AWS account, as in this scenario.\n\nStatement E is false: replication does not automatically apply Object Lock legal holds; Object Lock is a separate, explicitly configured feature and is not imposed as a side effect of replication.\n\nFor the exam, also know the CRR-versus-SRR distinction: both need versioning on both buckets and an IAM role, both are asynchronous and can be cross-account, but CRR crosses Regions (DR, latency, compliance) while SRR stays in one Region (log aggregation, in-Region compliance). Batch Replication also handles retries for previously failed replications.",
