    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "The requirement is cross-Region disaster recovery with very aggressive RPO/RTO. Aurora Global Database replicates data at the storage layer from a primary Region to one or more secondary Regions with a typical RPO of about one second and supports promoting a secondary Region in under a minute (RTO < 1 min). This directly satisfies surviving the loss of the entire primary Region within the stated objectives, while also providing low-latency local reads in the secondary Region.\n\nYou would not add more replicas or a larger standby within us-east-1 (B and C) because everything still resides in a single Region — an entire-Region outage takes them all down, so they cannot meet a cross-Region mandate no matter how many AZs are involved. Daily cross-Region snapshot copies (D) technically survive a Region loss but give an RPO measured in hours (up to a day of data loss) and a slow restore-based RTO, badly missing the ~1s RPO / <1min RTO targets.\n\nFor the exam, also know the distinction between Aurora Global Database (purpose-built, storage-level cross-Region replication, ~1s RPO, fast managed promotion) and a plain cross-Region Aurora read replica (also possible but with higher lag and slower, more manual promotion). Match single-AZ vs single-Region carefully: 'survive an AZ failure' = Multi-AZ; 'survive a Region failure' = cross-Region / Global Database.",
    "difficulty": 3,
    "tags": [
      "Aurora",
      "Aurora-Global-Database",
      "disaster-recovery",
      "RPO-RTO"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon Aurora > User Guide > Using Amazon Aurora global databases"
    ],
    "inShort": "Aurora Global Database replicates at the storage layer into a second Region with about 1 second of data loss and promotion in under a minute — the only option that survives losing us-east-1.",
    "trap": "More replicas or a bigger standby in us-east-1 (B/C): more AZs feels like more resilience, but everything is still in the one Region the mandate says must be able to disappear."
