    "explanation": "EC2 instance store is correct because it is physically attached NVMe/SSD on the host, giving the lowest latency and highest raw IOPS of any option. Because the scratch data is disposable, its main drawback — it is ephemeral and lost on stop, hibernate, or terminate (though it survives a reboot) — does not matter here.\n\nYou would not choose EBS gp3 because it is network-attached and persistent; you would be paying for durability you do not need and accepting slightly higher latency than local NVMe.\n\nYou would not choose EFS because it is a shared network file system reached over NFS, adding network overhead that a single-instance high-speed buffer does not want.\n\nYou would not choose S3 because it is object storage accessed by API, not a low-latency block device for scratch buffers.\n\nFor the exam, also know instance store vs EBS: instance store is fast but ephemeral and tied to the host, while EBS persists independently of the instance lifecycle and can be detached, snapshotted, and reattached — choose instance store only when data loss on stop/terminate is acceptable.",
