    "explanation": "io2 Block Express is correct because it is the only EBS volume type that delivers up to 256,000 IOPS, consistent sub-millisecond latency, and 99.999% durability — exactly what a mission-critical database demanding 120,000 IOPS requires.\n\nYou would not use gp3 because a single gp3 volume is capped at 80,000 IOPS and 2,000 MiB/s, so it cannot reach 120,000 IOPS on its own, and its durability is 99.8%–99.9% — well below io2 Block Express's 99.999%.\n\nYou would not use st1 volumes in RAID 0 because HDD-based st1 is optimized for sequential throughput, not the random IOPS or sub-millisecond latency a transactional database needs; striping HDDs also multiplies failure risk without meeting the latency target.\n\nYou would not use instance store in RAID 0 because instance store is ephemeral — data is lost on stop or terminate — so it cannot satisfy the 99.999% durability requirement no matter how it is striped.\n\nFor the exam, also know that there is no longer a separate lower-tier io2: as of April 30, 2025 all io2 volumes are io2 Block Express volumes, reaching 256,000 IOPS on Nitro-based instances (other instance types can attach volumes provisioned up to 64,000 IOPS but achieve up to 32,000), 4,000 MiB/s, and 64 TiB per volume with sub-millisecond latency. The older io1 type is the one that tops out at 64,000 IOPS and 1,000 MiB/s, and it carries only 99.8%–99.9% durability.",
