    "explanation": "io2 Block Express for the order database (A) is correct because it provides sub-millisecond latency, very high IOPS, and 99.999% durability on persistent, network-attached block storage — the right fit for a critical database that must survive instance restarts.\n\nEC2 instance store for the scratch tier (B) is correct because it is physically attached NVMe/SSD offering the lowest latency and highest raw IOPS, and its ephemeral nature is acceptable since the scratch data is rebuilt on every restart.\n\nYou would not use sc1 for the order database (C) because Cold HDD is the cheapest, lowest-performance tier meant for infrequently accessed data — it cannot deliver sub-millisecond block latency.\n\nYou would not use EFS for the scratch tier (D) because it is a shared NFS file system reached over the network, adding latency that a lowest-latency local scratch tier must avoid.\n\nYou would not use S3 Standard for the scratch tier (E) because object storage accessed by API is not a low-latency block device for high-frequency overflow buffers.\n\nFor the exam, also know the instance store vs EBS trade-off: instance store gives the best latency but is ephemeral and cannot be snapshotted, while EBS (especially io2 Block Express) trades a small amount of latency for persistence, durability, and snapshot-based backups.",
