    "explanation": "A Network Load Balancer is correct. Operating at Layer 4, the NLB handles millions of requests per second at ultra-low latency, scales instantly to spikes without pre-warming, preserves the client source IP, works with plain TCP/UDP, and provides a static IP address per Availability Zone (with optional Elastic IPs). That directly matches every requirement in the scenario.\n\nYou would not choose an Application Load Balancer because it is a Layer 7 balancer built for HTTP/HTTPS content routing; it adds request-processing latency, does not natively expose a static IP per AZ, and is not designed for raw TCP throughput at this scale. You would not choose the Classic Load Balancer because it is a previous-generation option AWS steers customers away from and lacks the NLB's performance and features. Global Accelerator is not itself a load balancer; it routes traffic over the AWS backbone to endpoints such as an NLB, so it complements rather than replaces one.\n\nFor the exam, contrast ALB vs. NLB: pick ALB for HTTP path/host-based routing and features like WebSockets and header inspection; pick NLB for extreme performance, low latency, static IPs, source-IP preservation, and non-HTTP TCP/UDP traffic.",
