    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "Using AWS Snowball Edge is correct. The Snow Family is designed for offline, petabyte-scale migration precisely when network bandwidth is the bottleneck: you copy data to a physical appliance and ship it to AWS, completing the transfer far faster than a slow link would allow. (For 200 TB you would order multiple Snowball Edge devices, since each provides on the order of tens of TB of usable capacity.) The key caveat tested here is that when Snowball imports data into S3, object names must comply with S3 object key naming rules — otherwise those objects fail to load.\n\nYou would not use AWS DataSync or Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration because both depend on the network path to AWS; over a slow, unreliable link, moving 200 TB online would take far too long. You would not use AWS Storage Gateway Tape Gateway because it is a virtual tape library for backup/archival workflows to S3/Glacier storage, not a bulk data-center migration tool.\n\nFor the exam, also know the online-vs-offline split: DataSync moves data over the network on a schedule; the Snow Family moves it offline via appliance. Snowball Edge devices offer high-speed network ports for fast local data loading.",
