    "explanation": "A Compute Savings Plan is correct. It is a commitment to spend a fixed dollar amount per hour for a 1- or 3-year term, and it automatically applies across any instance family, size, Region, OS, and tenancy. Uniquely, it also covers AWS Fargate and AWS Lambda usage. That makes it the only listed option that keeps discounting the company's spend as it migrates from EC2 to containers and serverless without repurchasing anything.\n\nYou would not choose an EC2 Instance Savings Plan because, while flexible on size within a family, it is locked to one instance family in one Region and does not cover Fargate or Lambda. Standard Reserved Instances give the deepest discount but are the least flexible and apply only to EC2, so a serverless migration would waste them. Convertible Reserved Instances allow attribute changes but still apply only to EC2 and require exchanges to adjust, and they too exclude Fargate and Lambda.\n\nFor the exam, also know the trade-off axis: Compute Savings Plan = maximum flexibility (covers Fargate/Lambda); EC2 Instance Savings Plan and Standard RIs = deeper discount, less flexibility. Remember Savings Plans and RIs are billing discounts, not capacity reservations.",
