    "explanation": "Option B is correct. A Compute Savings Plan (like any Savings Plan or a regional Reserved Instance) is a billing construct that lowers the rate you pay on eligible usage — it does not reserve physical capacity. To guarantee capacity in a specific Availability Zone, you create an On-Demand Capacity Reservation (or buy a zonal Reserved Instance), which sets aside capacity for your account in that AZ regardless of the discount you receive. This directly addresses insufficient-capacity errors during a high-demand event.\n\nYou would not assume the Savings Plan guarantees capacity (A): it never does, and while the On-Demand vCPU limit is a soft quota you can raise via Service Quotas, quota headroom does not reserve physical capacity in a constrained AZ. Converting to an EC2 Instance Savings Plan (C) is still just a billing discount and reserves nothing. Switching to Spot (D) is the opposite of a guarantee — Spot capacity can be reclaimed and offers no assurance during peak demand.\n\nFor the exam, also know the distinction: Savings Plans and regional RIs = billing discount and flexibility; zonal Reserved Instances and On-Demand Capacity Reservations = capacity guarantee in a specific AZ. Combine a Savings Plan (for the discount) with a Capacity Reservation (for the guarantee) when you need both.",
    "difficulty": 3,
