    "explanation": "Cross-account KMS access is always two-sided. The resource side is the key policy in Account A: a KMS key is only usable by an external account if the key policy explicitly allows that account or principal, so the role needs kms:Decrypt granted there (A). The identity side is Account B's own IAM policy: even when the key policy allows the whole external account, the administrator of Account B must still delegate the permission to the specific role by allowing kms:Decrypt on the Account A key ARN (B). Neither side alone authorizes the call — this is the same both-sides rule you apply to cross-account S3 and it is heavily tested for KMS.\n\nYou would not switch to SSE-S3 (C): it removes the key-permission problem only by removing the control you presumably chose SSE-KMS for — a per-key access policy and a CloudTrail record of every decrypt. It is a requirements change, not a fix, and SSE-S3 keys cannot be shared or audited per principal.\n\nYou would not enable automatic rotation (D): rotation changes backing material on a schedule and has no bearing on authorization.\n\nYou would not create the grant as described (E): a KMS grant is created by a principal in the key's own account (or one already permitted by the key policy), pointing at a grantee principal — Account B cannot originate a grant for a key it has no access to, so this does not bootstrap the permission.\n\nFor the exam, also know that only a customer managed key can be shared cross-account at all: AWS managed keys have a policy you cannot edit, which is why cross-account scenarios always force a customer managed key.",
    "difficulty": 3,
