    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "A KMS custom key store backed by an AWS CloudHSM cluster is exactly this scenario. The KMS key's material is generated and stored in HSMs in your own single-tenant cluster (FIPS 140-3 Level 3 validated, administered only by your crypto officers), but the key still presents a normal KMS key ARN. S3 SSE-KMS, EBS encryption, and RDS encryption keep calling KMS as before, so nothing in the application changes.\n\nYou would not use SSE-C because it only addresses S3 — it makes your application transmit the key on every HTTPS request and gives you no story at all for EBS or RDS, which have no SSE-C equivalent. It also does not put keys in dedicated HSMs; it just means AWS discards the key after each request.\n\nYou would not use imported key material (BYOK) because although you generate the key outside AWS, KMS still stores and uses that material in AWS's shared multi-tenant HSMs — it fails the dedicated single-tenant hardware requirement. Imported material also cannot use KMS automatic key rotation (you must import new material yourself), and if it expires or you delete it, every object encrypted under it becomes unreadable.\n\nYou would not call CloudHSM directly from the applications because that abandons the KMS integrations. S3, EBS, and RDS cannot encrypt with a raw CloudHSM key — they only accept KMS keys — so you would be rewriting code and losing managed encryption for exactly the services the requirement says to keep.\n\nFor the exam, also know the external key store (XKS) variant of a custom key store: it keeps key material in a key manager you run outside AWS entirely, and it is the answer when the requirement is that key material never resides in AWS in any form. Know the shared constraints too — custom key store keys are symmetric encryption keys only (no asymmetric, HMAC, imported material, automatic rotation, or multi-Region keys), and if your cluster or external key manager is unavailable, every KMS request against those keys fails and AWS cannot recover the keys for you. Note that FIPS level alone no longer separates KMS from CloudHSM: KMS HSMs have been FIPS 140-3 Level 3 validated since February 2025, so read these questions for tenancy and administrative control instead.",
    "difficulty": 3,
    "tags": [
      "kms",
      "cloudhsm",
      "custom-key-store",
      "encryption-at-rest",
      "compliance"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > AWS Key Management Service > Developer Guide > Custom key stores",
      "AWS Documentation > AWS CloudHSM > User Guide > Compliance validation for AWS CloudHSM"
    ],
    "inShort": "A CloudHSM-backed KMS custom key store gives single-tenant HSM custody while S3, EBS, and RDS keep using ordinary KMS keys.",
    "trap": "BYOK imported key material sounds like customer-owned keys, but KMS still stores it in shared multi-tenant HSMs, failing the dedicated-hardware rule."
