    "correct": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "explanation": "DynamoDB encrypts all tables at rest with no option to turn it off — the choice is only which KMS key protects them: an AWS owned key (the default, at no additional charge), an AWS managed key, or a customer managed key when you need a key policy and CloudTrail visibility of key usage. Lambda likewise encrypts environment variables at rest by default using an AWS managed key, but the console decrypts and displays the values for anyone with lambda:GetFunction-style read access. The fix the exam wants is to encrypt the sensitive value with a customer managed key (using the console's encryption helpers), withhold kms:Decrypt on that key from the support role, and have the function decrypt the value at runtime.\n\nOption C is wrong because DynamoDB has no unencrypted state to fix — the table has been encrypted since creation, so there is nothing to enable.\n\nOption D is wrong because EBS encryption by default is an EC2 block-storage account setting; it has no bearing on Lambda, whose environment variables are encrypted regardless.\n\nOption E is wrong because SQS encryption is not a create-time property: you can enable SSE-SQS or SSE-KMS on an existing queue at any time, and messages already in the queue simply are not retroactively encrypted. Queues created in the console now get SSE-SQS by default. The same 'enable it later' flexibility applies to SNS topics and Kinesis Data Streams.\n\nFor the exam, also know the sharper alternative for a third-party API key: keep it in AWS Secrets Manager or Parameter Store SecureString and fetch it at runtime rather than storing it in an environment variable at all — that removes the value from the function configuration entirely and, with Secrets Manager, adds rotation. Contrast this whole group with EFS, FSx, and RDS, where at-rest encryption is a create-time-only decision.",
