    "explanation": "FSx for NetApp ONTAP (A) is correct because it is the multi-protocol FSx flavor: a single ONTAP volume can be exported over NFS to the Linux pipeline and shared over SMB to the Windows analysts at the same time, with Active Directory integration for the SMB side. Both client populations read and write the same underlying copy of the data, which is exactly the no-duplication requirement.\n\nYou would not use FSx for Windows File Server (B) because it serves SMB only. The Windows analysts would be happy, but the Linux pipeline has no supported NFS path to the same data.\n\nYou would not use EFS (C) because EFS is an NFS-only file system for Linux clients. It has no SMB endpoint and no Active Directory-authenticated Windows share, so the analysts could not open the files.\n\nYou would not use FSx for Lustre (D) because Lustre is a Linux-only parallel file system built for HPC and ML throughput. It does not present an SMB share, and the S3 data repository association addresses data movement, not multi-protocol access.\n\nFor the exam, also know the rest of the FSx line-up so ONTAP distractors can be ruled in or out on sight: Windows File Server = SMB plus Active Directory; Lustre = highest-throughput HPC/ML on Linux; OpenZFS = NFS for Linux with ZFS snapshots and cloning; ONTAP = NFS + SMB + iSCSI together, plus NetApp features.",
