    "explanation": "Required concurrency equals requests per second multiplied by average duration: 800 x 1.5 = 1,200 concurrent executions steady-state, which is above the default 1,000 concurrent executions per Region. The account concurrency limit is a soft quota, so raising it through Service Quotas is the direct fix for 429 throttling on a synchronous API.\n\nYou would not add provisioned concurrency because it addresses cold-start latency, not the concurrency ceiling — provisioned concurrency is itself drawn from the same account limit, so 100 warm environments would not lift the 1,000 ceiling.\n\nYou would not buffer through SQS because the caller is a synchronous API expecting a response; a queue turns it into an asynchronous pattern and breaks the request-response contract users depend on.\n\nYou would not simply raise memory because, while a faster function shortens duration and therefore reduces concurrency, guessing at a speedup is indirect — and if the workload is I/O-bound waiting on a downstream call, extra CPU changes nothing.\n\nFor the exam, also know Lambda's scale-out behaviour: each function adds up to 1,000 concurrent executions every 10 seconds, so an instant spike far above current capacity can throttle even when the account limit is not yet reached.",
