    "id": "d4-storage-cost-14",
    "domainId": "d4",
    "topicId": "d4-storage-cost",
    "type": "single",
    "stem": "You are the architect at a manufacturing company. A factory-floor application writes to a 40 TB dataset held on iSCSI block volumes on-premises and requires low-latency local access to the entire dataset — a cloud round trip during a read would stall the production line. The company wants the data backed up to AWS without buying more on-premises storage capacity for backups, and wants point-in-time recovery. Which Storage Gateway configuration is appropriate?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Volume Gateway in stored mode, which keeps the full dataset on-premises and asynchronously backs it up to AWS as EBS snapshots"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Volume Gateway in cached mode, which keeps the primary data in Amazon S3 and caches only frequently accessed blocks on-premises"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "S3 File Gateway with an SMB share backed by S3 Standard and a lifecycle rule to S3 Standard-IA"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "FSx File Gateway fronting an Amazon FSx for Windows File Server file system"
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "Volume Gateway stored mode keeps the complete primary dataset on local disk, so every read of the 40 TB is served at on-premises latency with no cloud round trip — which is the hard requirement here. Stored volumes are asynchronously backed up to AWS and stored as EBS snapshots, giving exactly the point-in-time recovery the company wants, and the backup copies live in AWS rather than on additional on-premises hardware.\n\nYou would not use cached mode because cached volumes keep the primary data in S3 and hold only a hot subset locally. A read that misses the cache goes to AWS, which is precisely the stall the production line cannot tolerate. Cached mode is the right answer when the goal is to minimize on-premises storage; here the goal is full local low-latency access.\n\nYou would not use S3 File Gateway because the application writes to iSCSI block volumes, not an NFS or SMB file share. File Gateway maps files to S3 objects and cannot present block storage.\n\nYou would not use FSx File Gateway because it provides a low-latency on-premises cache in front of an FSx for Windows File Server SMB share — again a file protocol, not the iSCSI block interface the application requires.\n\nFor the exam, also know the memory hook for Volume Gateway modes: 'stored' means the data is stored with you (all of it local, backed up to AWS), 'cached' means the data is cached with you (all of it in AWS, hot subset local). The stem's phrasing about local disk capacity versus local latency is what picks between them — and note stored volumes are capped at 16 TiB each, so a 40 TB dataset spans multiple volumes.",
    "difficulty": 3,
    "tags": [
      "Volume Gateway",
      "cached vs stored",
      "iSCSI",
      "EBS snapshots",
      "hybrid storage"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > AWS Storage Gateway > Volume Gateway User Guide > How Volume Gateway works",
      "AWS Documentation > AWS Storage Gateway > Volume Gateway User Guide > Cached volumes and stored volumes"
    ],
    "inShort": "Stored volumes keep all 40 TB local for full low-latency reads while snapshotting to AWS for recovery.",
    "trap": "Cached mode also avoids buying backup disk, but its primary copy lives in S3 — cache misses trigger the round trip that stalls the line."
