    "explanation": "A and B are correct. AWS does not charge for data transfer IN from the internet, so the 200 TB upload costs nothing in transfer (you still pay S3 PUT requests and storage). And data transfer between S3 and EC2 in the SAME Region is free, so the in-Region processing reads cost nothing either. Those two facts are exactly why \"upload it online and process it in-Region\" is so often the low-cost migration answer.\n\nC is wrong: inbound transfer is free, not symmetric with outbound. Only data leaving AWS to the internet is billed per GB (after a small monthly free allowance).\n\nD is wrong: Cross-Region Replication bills per-GB data transfer OUT of the source Region plus replication PUT requests, regardless of account ownership. Same-account does not waive inter-Region transfer.\n\nE is wrong: the internet-out rate applies to bytes leaving AWS for the internet, not to an in-Region S3-to-EC2 read.\n\nFor the exam, also know that traffic crossing Availability Zones inside a Region is billed per GB in each direction — so a chatty multi-AZ design has a real transfer cost — and remember that replication is not a backup: CRR copies deletes and overwrites unless versioning plus delete-marker controls protect you.",
    "difficulty": 2,
