    "id": "d1-data-protection-33",
    "domainId": "d1",
    "topicId": "d1-data-protection",
    "type": "single",
    "stem": "You are the solutions architect for a media company delivering a global website through an Amazon CloudFront distribution, with an Application Load Balancer origin in the eu-west-1 Region. You requested a public certificate in AWS Certificate Manager in eu-west-1 for www.example.com, but it does not appear in the list of certificates you can attach to the CloudFront distribution. What must you do to serve HTTPS on your custom domain through CloudFront?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Request or import the certificate in AWS Certificate Manager in the us-east-1 Region, then attach it to the distribution"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Move the Application Load Balancer and its certificate to the us-east-1 Region"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Export the eu-west-1 certificate and import it into CloudFront's edge locations manually"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Enable SNI on the CloudFront distribution so it can use the eu-west-1 certificate"
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "CloudFront is a global service and only reads ACM certificates stored in the us-east-1 Region. To use a custom domain over HTTPS on CloudFront, the certificate for that domain must be requested or imported in us-east-1 and then selected on the distribution; a certificate in eu-west-1 will never appear as an option.\n\nYou would not move the ALB to us-east-1 (option B) because the origin can stay in eu-west-1 — only the CloudFront-facing certificate location matters, and the ALB keeps its own Regional certificate. You cannot hand-install a certificate on CloudFront edge locations (option C); CloudFront distributes the certificate to its edges for you, and the only supported input is an ACM certificate selected from us-east-1 (or an IAM-uploaded certificate). Enabling SNI (option D) lets one endpoint present multiple certificates but does not change which Region CloudFront reads certificates from.\n\nFor the exam, also know the mirror rule: Regional endpoints (ALB, NLB, Regional API Gateway) use an ACM certificate in their own Region, while CloudFront and edge-optimized API Gateway custom domains always read from us-east-1 — and for internal-only endpoints that public clients need not trust, you issue certificates from AWS Private CA instead of a public ACM certificate.",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "tags": [
      "ACM",
      "CloudFront",
      "TLS",
      "us-east-1"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon CloudFront > Developer Guide > Requirements for using SSL/TLS certificates with CloudFront",
      "AWS Documentation > AWS Certificate Manager > User Guide > Services integrated with AWS Certificate Manager"
    ],
    "inShort": "CloudFront only uses ACM certificates from us-east-1; Regional services use certificates stored in their own Region.",
    "trap": "Enabling SNI (D) does not change which Region CloudFront reads certificates from — it must be us-east-1."
