    "id": "d1-network-security-36",
    "domainId": "d1",
    "topicId": "d1-network-security",
    "type": "multi",
    "stem": "You are the security architect for a retail platform. AWS WAF is attached to your Amazon CloudFront distribution, but penetration testers demonstrate they can bypass CloudFront entirely by sending requests straight to the public Application Load Balancer, evading every WAF rule. You must ensure only your CloudFront distribution can reach the ALB. Which TWO actions together achieve this? (Choose TWO.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Restrict the ALB's security group inbound rules to the AWS-managed prefix list com.amazonaws.global.cloudfront.origin-facing"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Configure CloudFront to add a secret custom header to origin requests, and add an AWS WAF rule on the ALB that blocks any request missing that header value"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Replace the internet-facing ALB with an internal ALB and rely on CloudFront's default origin path to reach it"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Add a Network ACL deny rule for 0.0.0.0/0 on the ALB's subnet"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Enable AWS Shield Advanced on the ALB"
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "explanation": "Two controls combine to lock the ALB to CloudFront. First, restricting the ALB security group to the AWS-managed prefix list com.amazonaws.global.cloudfront.origin-facing ensures only CloudFront's origin-facing IP ranges can connect, and AWS keeps that prefix list current for you. Second, because that prefix list still admits any CloudFront distribution and not just yours, CloudFront adds a secret custom header on origin requests and a WAF rule on the ALB blocks any request that lacks the header — so only your distribution passes.\n\nYou would not make the ALB internal (option C) because a standard internet-facing CloudFront origin must be publicly resolvable and reachable; an internal ALB removes CloudFront's normal path to it. A Network ACL deny for 0.0.0.0/0 (option D) blocks CloudFront's own requests too, taking the site down. AWS Shield Advanced (option E) provides DDoS protection, response-team support, and cost protection, but does nothing to restrict which sources may reach the ALB.\n\nFor the exam, also know that CloudFront VPC origins is a newer feature that lets a distribution reach an ALB in a private subnet with no public exposure at all, but that is a different design from the prefix-list-plus-secret-header pattern tested here.",
    "difficulty": 3,
    "tags": [
      "CloudFront",
      "ALB",
      "WAF",
      "prefix list"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon CloudFront > Developer Guide > Restrict access to an Application Load Balancer",
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon VPC > User Guide > Work with AWS-managed prefix lists"
    ],
    "inShort": "Lock the ALB security group to CloudFront's origin-facing prefix list and add a secret header verified by WAF.",
    "trap": "The managed prefix list alone (A) still admits every CloudFront distribution; the secret header (B) is what limits access to yours."
