    "id": "d1-network-security-45",
    "domainId": "d1",
    "topicId": "d1-network-security",
    "type": "multi",
    "stem": "A security review of your VPC finds that every EC2 Security Group still has the default outbound rule allowing all traffic to 0.0.0.0/0. Your CISO worries that a compromised instance could exfiltrate data or connect to an external command-and-control server. You must minimize the outbound attack surface while still letting instances reach only Amazon S3 and one specific partner API whose destinations are expressed as domain names. Which TWO actions should you take? (Choose TWO.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Remove the default allow-all egress rule and add outbound rules permitting only the required destinations and ports"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Use AWS Network Firewall for domain-based (FQDN) outbound filtering where destinations cannot be pinned to fixed IP addresses"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Rely on the Security Groups' stateful inbound rules to block the exfiltration traffic"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Add an inbound NACL deny rule for 0.0.0.0/0 on the instance subnets"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Enable AWS Shield Advanced to terminate the outbound connection"
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "explanation": "The default Security Group egress rule allows all outbound traffic, so the first control is to remove it and permit only the specific destinations and ports the workload needs — this directly shrinks the exfiltration path. Because Security Groups match only IP/CIDR, port, prefix list, or another security group (never domain names), the partner API expressed as domain names requires AWS Network Firewall, which supports FQDN/domain-based outbound filtering. Together these lock egress down to only what is allowed.\n\nYou would not rely on inbound Security Group rules (option C) because exfiltration is an outbound-initiated flow, and inbound rules govern the opposite direction. An inbound NACL deny (option D) likewise only affects inbound traffic and does nothing to stop an instance from sending data out. AWS Shield Advanced (option E) is a DDoS mitigation service for inbound attack traffic, not an outbound egress control.\n\nFor the exam, remember the default posture of a new Security Group: no inbound rules, all outbound allowed. 'Prevent an instance from exfiltrating data or calling home' is an egress-filtering question answered by restricting SG outbound rules plus Network Firewall for domain-based control; for S3 specifically, a gateway VPC endpoint with an endpoint policy keeps that traffic off the internet entirely.",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "tags": [
      "Security Groups",
      "egress filtering",
      "AWS Network Firewall",
      "data exfiltration"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon VPC > User Guide > Security group rules",
      "AWS Documentation > AWS Network Firewall > Developer Guide > Stateful domain list rule groups"
    ],
    "inShort": "New Security Groups allow all outbound by default; restrict egress rules (and use Network Firewall for domains) to stop exfiltration.",
    "trap": "Inbound controls (C/D) do not stop exfiltration — it is an outbound flow, blocked only by egress rules and Network Firewall."
