    "id": "d1-network-security-47",
    "domainId": "d1",
    "topicId": "d1-network-security",
    "type": "single",
    "stem": "A development team exposed an internal reporting function through a Lambda function URL that was created with AuthType NONE, so anyone who learns the URL can invoke it. Security now requires that only one specific IAM role, owned by another AWS account, be able to call the function, and that every request be cryptographically signed by the caller. What should you do?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Set the function URL AuthType to AWS_IAM and grant lambda:InvokeFunctionUrl to that role in the function's resource-based policy, so callers must send SigV4-signed requests"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Keep AuthType NONE and validate a shared secret header inside the function code"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Attach a Security Group to the function URL that allows only the partner account's IP range"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Configure an Amazon Cognito user pool authorizer directly on the function URL"
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "Lambda function URLs support exactly two auth types. AWS_IAM requires the caller to sign the request with SigV4 and to be authorized for the lambda:InvokeFunctionUrl action; for a cross-account caller you must also grant that action to the role in the function's resource-based policy. That gives you the signed, identity-scoped access the requirement asks for.\n\nYou would not keep AuthType NONE with a secret header (option B) because the URL stays publicly invokable — the function still executes and bills on every unauthenticated request, and a static secret is not a signed, IAM-authorized call. Security Groups (option C) cannot be attached to a Lambda function URL; the URL is a public AWS-managed HTTPS endpoint, not an ENI in your VPC. A Cognito user pool authorizer (option D) is an API Gateway feature; function URLs have no authorizer concept beyond NONE and AWS_IAM.\n\nFor the exam, contrast this with API Gateway: put an API in front of Lambda when you need Cognito or Lambda authorizers, throttling, usage plans, WAF, or a private (VPC-only) endpoint; a function URL with AWS_IAM is the minimal, no-extra-service option when simple signed HTTPS invocation is enough.",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "tags": [
      "AWS Lambda",
      "function URL",
      "AWS_IAM auth",
      "resource-based policy"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > AWS Lambda > Developer Guide > Security and auth model for Lambda function URLs",
      "AWS Documentation > AWS Lambda > Developer Guide > Using resource-based policies for Lambda"
    ],
    "inShort": "Lambda function URLs offer only NONE or AWS_IAM; AWS_IAM plus a resource-policy grant enforces signed, role-scoped invocation.",
    "trap": "A secret header with AuthType NONE (B) leaves the URL publicly invokable and billable — it is not IAM authorization."
