    "id": "d1-network-security-48",
    "domainId": "d1",
    "topicId": "d1-network-security",
    "type": "single",
    "stem": "Your company exposes a public HTTPS API behind a Network Load Balancer because a partner firewall-allowlists the NLB's static IP addresses. The security team now wants AWS WAF managed rule groups and a rate-based rule applied to this API, but WAF will not associate with the NLB. Which change adds WAF protection while keeping the existing static IP addresses?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Register an Application Load Balancer as the target of the NLB and associate the AWS WAF web ACL with that Application Load Balancer"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Associate the AWS WAF web ACL directly with the NLB's target group"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Replace WAF with a Security Group on the NLB that blocks abusive source IP addresses"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Enable AWS Shield Advanced on the NLB, which applies the same Layer 7 managed rule groups as AWS WAF"
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "AWS WAF web ACLs can be associated only with Amazon CloudFront, Application Load Balancers, API Gateway REST APIs, AppSync GraphQL APIs, Cognito user pools, App Runner services, and Verified Access instances — never with a Network Load Balancer, which operates at Layer 4. Because an ALB can be registered as an NLB target (target type 'alb'), you can keep the NLB's static IPs at the front, hand traffic to an internal ALB, and attach the web ACL to that ALB so managed and rate-based rules run on the HTTP requests.\n\nOption B is not a supported association: neither the NLB nor its target group can hold a web ACL. Option C is Layer 3/4 filtering by IP and port — a Security Group cannot evaluate HTTP content, run managed rule groups, or rate-limit per client. Option D confuses the products: Shield Advanced adds enhanced L3/L4 DDoS detection, Shield Response Team access, and DDoS cost protection (it includes WAF at no extra cost on supported resources), but it does not inspect HTTP payloads and does not make WAF attachable to an NLB.\n\nAdjacent-service note: a CloudFront distribution in front of the NLB is the other WAF-capable option, but it changes the client-facing IP model, so it does not satisfy a static-IP allowlist. AWS Global Accelerator gives static anycast IPs yet cannot hold a web ACL either; for genuinely non-HTTP TCP/UDP traffic the answers are AWS Shield and AWS Network Firewall.",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "tags": [
      "AWS WAF",
      "Network Load Balancer",
      "Application Load Balancer",
      "Layer 7"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > AWS WAF > Developer Guide > Associating a web ACL with an AWS resource",
      "AWS Documentation > Elastic Load Balancing > Network Load Balancers > Target groups > Application Load Balancer as a target"
    ],
    "inShort": "WAF never attaches to an NLB; put an ALB or CloudFront in the path instead.",
    "trap": "Attaching the web ACL to the NLB target group (B) is unsupported; Shield Advanced (D) is L3/L4 DDoS, not L7 rules."
