    "id": "d1-data-protection-35",
    "domainId": "d1",
    "topicId": "d1-data-protection",
    "type": "single",
    "stem": "You are a solutions architect at a genomics company. You just enabled Cross-Region Replication on an existing 60 TB Amazon S3 bucket (about 12 million objects) to a bucket in another Region for disaster recovery. A day later the destination holds only the handful of objects uploaded since you turned the rule on; the original 12 million are missing. What is the MOST appropriate way to get the pre-existing objects replicated?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Run an S3 Batch Replication job to replicate the pre-existing objects to the destination bucket"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Toggle the replication rule off and back on to force S3 to re-scan the whole bucket"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Enable S3 Replication Time Control (RTC) so the backlog replicates within the 15-minute SLA"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Switch to S3 Same-Region Replication, which copies existing objects automatically"
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "A live S3 replication rule acts only on objects written after it is enabled, so the 12 million pre-existing objects were never queued. S3 Batch Replication is the purpose-built, on-demand job that replicates objects that already exist (and objects that previously failed replication), producing a completion report — exactly what backfills the historical 60 TB.\n\nYou would not toggle the rule off and on: the live rule never reconsiders objects that predate it, no matter how many times you re-enable it, so nothing changes. You would not rely on Replication Time Control because RTC only adds a 15-minute SLA and CloudWatch metrics to the ongoing replication of NEW objects; it never reaches back to copy pre-existing ones. You would not switch to Same-Region Replication because SRR behaves identically on this point — it also copies only new objects and requires versioning — and moving the copy in-Region would defeat your cross-Region DR goal.\n\nFor the exam, also know that both source and destination buckets must have Versioning enabled for any replication to work, that replication is asynchronous (the PUT succeeds before the replica is created), and that the adjacent one-time bulk mover for non-versioned or ad-hoc copies is S3 Batch Operations Copy, while AWS DataSync is the answer when the source is on-premises rather than another S3 bucket.",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "tags": [
      "S3",
      "replication",
      "Batch Replication",
      "CRR",
      "versioning"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon S3 > User Guide > Replicating existing objects with S3 Batch Replication",
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon S3 > User Guide > Replicating objects within and across Regions"
    ],
    "inShort": "Live replication rules ignore pre-existing objects; S3 Batch Replication is the on-demand job that backfills them.",
    "trap": "RTC sounds like a fix, but its 15-minute SLA applies only to newly written objects, never to the pre-existing backlog."
