    "id": "d1-data-protection-38",
    "domainId": "d1",
    "topicId": "d1-data-protection",
    "type": "single",
    "stem": "A contractor's project is wrapping up. You want to immediately block all use of the customer managed KMS key it used while you confirm that nothing in production still depends on it. The change must be instantly reversible if something breaks, and under no circumstances may the encrypted data become unrecoverable. What is the MOST appropriate action?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Disable the KMS key — it instantly blocks all cryptographic use, is reversible at any time, and destroys no data"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Schedule the key for deletion with a 30-day waiting period and cancel it if something breaks"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Delete the key immediately; the ciphertext can later be recovered from the S3 or EBS data if needed"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Delete the key's alias so applications can no longer resolve the key"
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "Disabling a KMS key takes effect immediately, blocks every encrypt and decrypt call, and can be re-enabled at any moment with no data loss — precisely the reversible, non-destructive pause you want while validating that the key is truly unused.\n\nYou would not schedule deletion, because that starts a destructive 7-to-30-day countdown; even though you can cancel it, choosing a path whose end state is permanent data loss is unnecessary when Disable achieves the same block reversibly. You would not delete the key immediately — KMS offers no immediate-delete option (a minimum 7-day waiting period is always enforced), and once a key is deleted its ciphertext is permanently unrecoverable because AWS keeps no copy of the key material. You would not delete the alias, because that removes only a friendly pointer; applications referencing the key by ID or ARN keep working, so it neither reliably blocks use nor is the intended control.\n\nFor the exam, also know the adjacent nuances: AWS managed keys such as aws/s3 and aws/ebs cannot be disabled or deleted by you at all, and CloudTrail plus the KMS console's key-usage view are how you prove a key is genuinely idle before you act.",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "tags": [
      "KMS",
      "disable key",
      "key deletion",
      "aliases"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > AWS Key Management Service > Developer Guide > Enabling and disabling AWS KMS keys",
      "AWS Documentation > AWS Key Management Service > Developer Guide > Deleting AWS KMS keys"
    ],
    "inShort": "Disable is the instant, reversible way to block a KMS key; scheduling deletion risks permanent, unrecoverable data loss.",
    "trap": "Scheduling deletion with a cancel option seems safe, but it starts a countdown that ends in permanent loss; Disable blocks use with zero risk."
