    "explanation": "Enabling CloudTrail S3 data events for the bucket is correct. Object-level API calls (GetObject, PutObject, DeleteObject) are data events, which are off by default and must be explicitly turned on; once enabled, each object read and write is recorded with caller identity, key, and time — exactly what the auditors want.\n\nYou would not rely on the existing KMS Decrypt entries because those are management events on the KMS key: they prove a key was used, not which object was read, and they miss reads of any object not encrypted with that key. 'Log every key use' is not the same as 'log every object read.'\n\nYou would not make S3 server access logging the compliance system of record because it is best-effort and delayed by hours, with less structured records; it is a cheaper telemetry option, not the reliable auditable trail the requirement demands.\n\nYou would not use GuardDuty S3 protection because it analyzes CloudTrail S3 data events to detect threats — it is a detection layer that depends on data events being enabled, and it is not itself the object-access audit log.\n\nFor the exam, also know the split: management events = control-plane (bucket/key configuration), one free copy; data events = object-plane (individual reads/writes), off by default and billed per event.",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "tags": [
      "cloudtrail",
      "data-events",
      "management-events",
      "s3-object-logging",
      "audit"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > AWS CloudTrail > User Guide > Logging data events"
    ],
    "inShort": "S3 object reads/writes are CloudTrail data events — off by default; management events never record them.",
    "trap": "KMS Decrypt entries tempt you into thinking object access is logged, but they prove key use, not which object was read."
