    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "Log file integrity validation is correct. When enabled, CloudTrail creates hashed and RSA-signed digest files referencing each delivered log file; running 'aws cloudtrail validate-logs' later confirms whether any log file was modified, deleted, or forged after delivery — exactly the tamper-evidence the auditor wants.\n\nYou would not rely on SSE-KMS encryption of the bucket because encryption protects confidentiality (who can read the logs), not integrity; it does not prove the files were never altered after delivery.\n\nYou would not use CloudTrail Insights because it detects unusual API activity patterns (spikes in call volume or error rates); it is an anomaly-detection feature, not a proof-of-integrity mechanism.\n\nYou would not convert to a multi-Region trail because that only ensures events from all Regions are captured in one trail; it says nothing about whether delivered files were tampered with.\n\nFor the exam, pair integrity validation with an immutable destination: the digest files prove tampering happened, and S3 Object Lock prevents it from happening in the first place.",
    "difficulty": 2,
