    "explanation": "FSx for Lustre scratch file systems are optimized for temporary, high-throughput processing and are not replicated; if a file server fails, that data is lost by design. Durable data belongs on a persistent Lustre file system (which replicates data within its AZ and automatically replaces failed servers) or should be backed by and re-hydrated from an Amazon S3 data repository.\n\nYou would not choose B because the data loss is expected behavior for scratch, not a defect. You would not choose C because EFS One Zone is itself single-AZ (not durable across AZs), and it does not match the Lustre HPC throughput profile. You would not choose D because FSx for Lustre has no Multi-AZ deployment type at all; durability comes from choosing persistent deployment or S3 backing.\n\nFor the exam, also know that FSx for Windows File Server and FSx for NetApp ONTAP offer Multi-AZ deployment types that fail over across AZs — a different resilience model from Lustre's scratch-vs-persistent choice.",
    "difficulty": 2,
