    "id": "d3-storage-perf-29",
    "domainId": "d3",
    "topicId": "d3-storage-perf",
    "type": "single",
    "stem": "A media startup created an Amazon EFS file system with the Max I/O performance mode because an engineer assumed it was simply the 'faster' option. The file system now backs a latency-sensitive content management system running on a handful of EC2 instances, and users complain that individual page loads feel sluggish even though total throughput is nowhere near any limit. You are asked for the MOST accurate explanation and fix.",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Max I/O trades higher aggregate scale for higher per-operation latency; for this small, latency-sensitive workload, recreate the file system in General Purpose mode, since performance mode cannot be changed in place"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Max I/O has exhausted its burst credits; switch it to Provisioned throughput to restore low latency"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "The file system is in the Infrequent Access tier; disable lifecycle management to lower latency"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Simply change the performance mode from Max I/O to General Purpose on the existing file system to remove the added latency"
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "Option A is correct. Max I/O buys a higher aggregate ceiling at the cost of higher latency on each metadata and file operation, so on a small fleet that never approaches that ceiling it delivers no benefit and every operation simply feels slower — the classic conflation of 'more performance' with 'lower latency.' The fix is General Purpose mode, and because performance mode is fixed at creation, that means creating a new file system and migrating the data rather than flipping a switch.\n\nYou would not blame burst credits (B) because credit exhaustion is a Bursting throughput-mode behavior, not a performance-mode one. It would appear as a throughput cliff, not a steady per-operation latency penalty, and Provisioned throughput does not change per-operation latency.\n\nYou would not blame the IA tier (C) because Infrequent Access affects only cold, rarely accessed files and adds first-byte latency to those specific reads, not uniform sluggishness across an actively used CMS.\n\nYou would not choose an in-place mode change (D) because EFS performance mode is immutable after the file system is created; there is no toggle, so the only path is a new General Purpose file system plus a migration.\n\nFor the exam, also know the mental model: General Purpose equals lowest latency and is the right default for almost everything; Max I/O is a legacy mode for massively parallel aggregate scale with a latency penalty, and it is unavailable with Elastic throughput; and the mode is a create-time decision you cannot undo without a new file system.",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "tags": [
      "EFS",
      "performance-mode",
      "latency",
      "general-purpose"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon EFS > User Guide > Amazon EFS performance > Performance modes"
    ],
    "inShort": "General Purpose gives lowest latency; Max I/O adds latency, helps only massive fleets, and is fixed at creation.",
