    "explanation": "AWS Outposts is correct. Outposts puts AWS-managed infrastructure — EC2 and EBS — physically inside the plant, so the control loop runs locally at single-digit-millisecond latency while the team keeps the exact AWS APIs and tooling they use in the Region. This is precisely the low-latency local-processing use case Outposts exists for.\n\nYou would not use a Local Zone because it sits in a shared AWS metro facility, not on the factory floor; the network hop to that facility still adds latency the control loop cannot tolerate, and the compute is not on-premises.\n\nYou would not use Storage Gateway because it is a storage service only — it caches data locally but the actual image processing would still run in the Region, which is the very latency you must eliminate.\n\nYou would not rely on Direct Connect because it only makes the link to the Region faster and more consistent; bandwidth does not remove distance, so the compute still lives 60 ms away and cannot serve a single-digit-millisecond control loop.\n\nFor the exam, also know that Outposts is equally the answer for data-residency requirements — when regulation forces data to physically stay in a specific building or country while the application keeps using AWS APIs — and that AWS Local Zones are the answer only when 'near the users in a metro area' is enough.",
