    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "Increasing throughput capacity is correct. On FSx for Windows, storage capacity and throughput capacity are independent dials. Throughput capacity governs the MB/s the file system can push and the amount of memory used for caching, so raising it is the direct fix for a share that is performance-bound while still having free space — and it does not require rebuilding the file system.\n\nYou would not increase storage capacity because on an SSD-backed FSx for Windows file system throughput is not tied to how much space you provision; adding storage would spend money on capacity you already have a surplus of and would not raise throughput.\n\nYou would not switch to HDD because HDD storage is lower performance than SSD, not higher — moving to HDD would make a performance problem worse, not better.\n\nYou would not recreate the file system as Multi-AZ for performance because Multi-AZ is an availability feature (a standby in a second AZ); it is not a throughput multiplier, and it forces a rebuild the requirement rules out.\n\nFor the exam, also know that throughput capacity can be adjusted both up and down after creation, but the change is briefly disruptive — a few minutes of unavailability on Single-AZ, or an automatic failover on Multi-AZ — whereas storage-capacity increases happen in the background with no interruption.",
