    "explanation": "Migrating to gp3 (A) is correct and cheapest. gp3 gives every volume a baseline of 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s at no extra charge regardless of size, so a 100 GiB gp3 volume sustains 3,000 IOPS without buying capacity you do not need — and gp3 is typically about 20% cheaper per GB-month than gp2.\n\nYou would not grow the gp2 volume to 1,000 GiB (B) because although 3 IOPS/GiB x 1,000 GiB does reach a 3,000 IOPS baseline, it forces you to pay for 900 GiB of unused capacity purely to buy IOPS — the definition of over-provisioning the stem rules out.\n\nYou would not move to io2 Block Express (C) because, while it easily sustains 3,000 IOPS, it is the premium Provisioned IOPS tier priced for mission-critical workloads that genuinely need its consistent sub-millisecond latency and 99.999% durability. This stem asks for neither, so you would be paying io2's per-GiB and per-provisioned-IOPS premium for attributes the workload does not require — far more expensive than gp3 for this modest requirement.\n\nYou would not enable EBS-optimized (D) because it only guarantees dedicated instance-to-EBS bandwidth; it does not change a volume's provisioned or baseline IOPS, and on current Nitro instances it is already on by default.\n\nFor the exam, also know the decoupling gp3 introduced: gp2 ties IOPS to size, while gp3 lets you set IOPS and throughput independently of capacity — up to 80,000 IOPS and 2,000 MiB/s per volume (raised in September 2025 from the older 16,000 IOPS / 1,000 MiB/s limits), subject only to loose guardrails: you cannot exceed 500 IOPS per GiB, so the 80,000 IOPS ceiling needs a volume of at least 160 GiB, and throughput scales at 0.25 MiB/s per provisioned IOPS, so 2,000 MiB/s needs at least 8,000 provisioned IOPS. gp3 volumes also range from 1 GiB to 64 TiB. That flexibility, on top of the free 3,000 IOPS / 125 MiB/s floor, is why gp3 is the default general-purpose SSD. One nuance worth carrying into the exam: gp3 delivers single-digit-millisecond latency — consistent sub-millisecond latency is an io2 Block Express property, not a gp3 one.",
