    "explanation": "io2 Block Express (A) is the only type that satisfies every requirement. Since April 2025 all io2 volumes are io2 Block Express: 4 GiB to 64 TiB, up to 256,000 IOPS at a 1,000:1 IOPS-to-GiB ratio, up to 4,000 MiB/s, 99.999% durability, and — the deciding factor here — consistent sub-millisecond latency. A single 25 TiB volume covers the 40,000 IOPS asked for with room to spare (at 1,000:1 that ratio needs only 40 GiB), though sustaining more than roughly 32,000 IOPS requires a Nitro-based instance.\n\nYou would not use gp3 (B), but the reason is latency, not size or IOPS. Since the September 2025 ceiling increase, gp3 reaches 64 TiB and 80,000 provisioned IOPS at 500 IOPS per GiB (so 40,000 IOPS needs at least 80 GiB), which means a single 25 TiB volume at 40,000 IOPS is genuinely inside gp3's limits. What gp3 cannot promise is the latency profile: gp3 is a single-digit millisecond volume type, and only io2 Block Express offers consistent sub-millisecond latency. gp3 durability is also 99.8%–99.9% against io2's 99.999%.\n\nYou would not use io1 (C) because, although it reaches 64,000 IOPS and can handle 40,000 IOPS, its maximum volume size is still 16 TiB, so it cannot hold 25 TiB in a single volume. This is the classic trap: it clears the IOPS bar but fails on size.\n\nYou would not use st1 (D) because it is a throughput-optimized HDD built for large sequential streaming; it also caps at 16 TiB and cannot deliver 40,000 random IOPS anywhere near sub-millisecond latency.\n\nFor the exam, know the current size ceilings by type: gp2, io1, st1, and sc1 stop at 16 TiB, while gp3 and io2 Block Express both reach 64 TiB. So 'a single volume larger than 16 TiB' no longer points straight at io2 Block Express — you separate gp3 from io2 on the performance words instead: consistent sub-millisecond latency, 99.999% durability, or more than 80,000 IOPS mean io2 Block Express; anything softer than that usually means the cheaper gp3.",
