    "id": "d3-networking-perf-27",
    "domainId": "d3",
    "topicId": "d3-networking-perf",
    "type": "single",
    "stem": "You run an application behind AWS Global Accelerator with endpoint groups in us-east-1 and eu-west-1. You are deploying a risky change to the us-east-1 endpoints and want to send only 10% of the traffic that would normally go to us-east-1 there, letting the rest fall through to eu-west-1, then ramp up as confidence grows — while keeping the accelerator's two static anycast IPs. Which Global Accelerator control does this?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Set the us-east-1 endpoint group's traffic dial to 10%."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Set the endpoint weights on the us-east-1 endpoints to 10."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Put a Route 53 weighted record set in front of the accelerator splitting traffic 10/90."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Enable client affinity set to source IP on the accelerator's listener."
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "The traffic dial is a per-endpoint-group percentage (0-100%) that limits how much of the traffic that would otherwise route to that group is actually sent there. Setting the us-east-1 group's dial to 10% sends only a tenth of would-be us-east-1 traffic to us-east-1 and lets the rest flow to the next-closest healthy group (eu-west-1). Raising the dial over time ramps the Region back up — the built-in way to do gradual regional shifts and blue/green cutovers while keeping the accelerator's static anycast IPs.\n\nYou would not use endpoint weights, because weights split traffic among the endpoints inside one endpoint group, not across Regions; weighting the us-east-1 endpoints does not move traffic to eu-west-1.\n\nYou would not front the accelerator with a Route 53 weighted record, because that reintroduces DNS-based splitting with client-side caching and undoes the benefit of Global Accelerator's static IPs and backbone routing — the traffic dial achieves the shift natively.\n\nYou would not enable source-IP client affinity, because that is a stickiness control that pins a client to one endpoint; it does not shift a percentage of traffic away from a Region.\n\nFor the exam, also know the distinction from Route 53 weighted records: the traffic dial and endpoint weights are Global Accelerator's own controls, with the dial operating per Region and weights operating within a group.",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "tags": [
      "global-accelerator",
      "traffic-dial",
      "blue-green",
      "endpoint-group",
      "regional-shift"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > AWS Global Accelerator > Developer Guide > Adjusting traffic flow with traffic dials"
    ],
    "inShort": "A traffic dial caps the percentage of traffic to a whole endpoint group, shifting the rest to the next-closest Region.",
    "trap": "Endpoint weights also adjust proportions, but they split traffic among endpoints within one group — only the traffic dial shifts traffic away from a whole Region."
