    "explanation": "By default Global Accelerator uses client affinity 'none,' which routes on a 5-tuple flow hash, so a client's different connections can land on different endpoints. Setting client affinity to 'source IP' makes the accelerator send all connections from a given source IP to the same endpoint, which is the stickiness these calls need across both TCP signaling and UDP media.\n\nYou would not raise an endpoint weight, because weights control how traffic is distributed among endpoints in a group, not whether a given client sticks to one endpoint.\n\nYou would not change the traffic dial, because the dial caps the percentage of traffic sent to a whole endpoint group; it has nothing to do with pinning a client to an endpoint.\n\nYou would not enable cookie-based stickiness, because that is an Application Load Balancer feature. Global Accelerator operates at Layer 4 and has no HTTP cookies; its stickiness mechanism is source-IP client affinity.\n\nFor the exam, also know that the default client affinity 'none' uses a 5-tuple hash (source IP, source port, destination IP, destination port, protocol), while 'source IP' uses a 2-tuple hash of source IP and destination IP, which is what preserves a session to one endpoint.",
