    "explanation": "A public virtual interface advertises AWS public IP prefixes to your router and lets on-premises systems reach AWS public service endpoints such as S3, SQS, and DynamoDB in all AWS Regions over the Direct Connect circuit instead of the internet. Since no VPC resources are involved and multiple Regions are in scope, this is the simplest and most appropriate configuration.\n\nYou would not create a private virtual interface with a Direct Connect gateway because private VIFs carry traffic only to private IP space in VPCs. You would then have to build interface endpoints in every VPC and manage DNS, which is unnecessary work when no VPC resources are needed.\n\nYou would not create a transit virtual interface because transit VIFs exist to connect Direct Connect gateways to Transit Gateways for VPC-to-on-premises routing at scale, which is again a private-IP path with no relevance to public service endpoints.\n\nYou would not build a Site-to-Site VPN over Direct Connect here; that pattern exists to add IPsec encryption to public VIF traffic and introduces per-tunnel throughput limits of about 1.25 Gbps plus extra complexity the requirement does not call for.\n\nFor the exam, also know that a public VIF requires public IPv4 addresses for the BGP peering and for any prefixes you advertise to AWS (the BGP ASN itself may be public or private, and a public ASN must be one you own), and that a single Direct Connect connection can host public, private, and transit virtual interfaces at the same time, subject to the per-connection VIF limits.",
