    "explanation": "S3 Storage Class Analysis exists for exactly this decision. Enabled per bucket, prefix, or tag, it observes access over time and reports how much of your storage is infrequently accessed at each object age, so you can set the Lifecycle transition day with evidence instead of a guess. Analysis output can be exported to a CSV in S3 and visualized, but it never moves data itself — you still author the Lifecycle rule.\n\nYou would not use S3 Inventory because it is a scheduled flat-file listing of objects and metadata such as size, storage class, encryption, and replication status. It tells you what you have, not how often anything is read.\n\nYou would not use server access logging with Athena because, while technically capable of counting requests, it means enabling verbose logging on 900 TB of traffic, storing and scanning those logs, and building the age analysis yourself — a managed feature already does it.\n\nYou would not use Cost Explorer because it reports spend by service and usage type at the bucket level at best. It shows that storage costs money; it cannot tell you which objects stopped being read after 45 days.\n\nFor the exam, also know the sibling tools: S3 Storage Lens is the answer when the question asks for storage visibility and recommendations across an entire organization rather than one bucket, and Storage Class Analysis does not produce transition recommendations for One Zone-IA or the Glacier storage classes — it is scoped to justifying a move from Standard to Standard-IA.",
