    "explanation": "AWS Trusted Advisor inspects resource configuration and utilization and, in its Cost Optimization category, reports exactly these findings: underutilized or unattached Amazon EBS volumes, unassociated Elastic IP addresses, idle load balancers, and idle RDS instances. Your Enterprise Support plan unlocks the full check set, so this is the fastest AWS-native path to a deletion list.\n\nYou would not use Compute Optimizer because it right-sizes resources that are actively in use, based on CloudWatch metrics. A volume attached to nothing generates no meaningful utilization data, so it is Trusted Advisor's territory, not Compute Optimizer's.\n\nYou would not use AWS Budgets because budget actions apply IAM policies, Service Control Policies, or stop EC2/RDS instances when a spending threshold is crossed. Budgets react to dollar amounts; they do not enumerate orphaned resources.\n\nYou would not use the Cost and Usage Report because it gives you the most granular billing line items available, but it tells you that a volume ID incurred charges — not that the volume is unattached. Determining waste from CUR alone would require you to join it against resource state yourself.\n\nFor the exam, also know that EBS snapshots are billed separately from volumes and survive the deletion of their source volume, so orphaned snapshots keep accruing charges. Use Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager retention rules to age them out automatically rather than relying on manual cleanup.",
    "difficulty": 2,
