    "id": "d4-compute-cost-31",
    "domainId": "d4",
    "topicId": "d4-compute-cost",
    "type": "single",
    "stem": "You are the solutions architect for a healthcare ISV migrating a Windows Server and SQL Server Enterprise application to AWS. The company already owns perpetual licenses that are sold per physical socket and per physical core, and the license auditor requires evidence of the exact physical server, socket count, and core count the software runs on. The workload is a steady 24/7 fleet of eight instances. Which EC2 purchasing and tenancy option MOST cost-effectively lets the company reuse the licenses it already owns?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Run the instances on EC2 Dedicated Hosts and purchase a 3-year Dedicated Host Reservation"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Run the instances as EC2 Dedicated Instances and purchase 3-year Standard Reserved Instances"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Run the instances on shared tenancy and purchase a 3-year Compute Savings Plan"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Run the instances on shared tenancy inside a cluster placement group with On-Demand Capacity Reservations"
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "Dedicated Hosts allocate a whole physical server to your account and expose the host ID, socket count, and physical core count. That visibility is exactly what socket- and core-based BYOL agreements for Windows Server and SQL Server Enterprise require, and it lets you place multiple instances on one host under a single license. Because the workload is steady 24/7, a 3-year Dedicated Host Reservation drops the hourly host rate substantially, making this the cheapest compliant option.\n\nYou would not use Dedicated Instances because, although they guarantee your instances do not share hardware with other AWS customers, they give you no view of the underlying host, its sockets, or its cores, and you cannot control instance placement across stop/start cycles. That defeats socket/core BYOL reporting, so you would have to buy license-included instances instead and pay again for licenses you already own.\n\nYou would not use shared tenancy with a Compute Savings Plan because shared tenancy runs your instances on multiplexed hardware with no physical-server affinity at all. The Savings Plan is a good billing construct, but it does nothing for licensing compliance here.\n\nYou would not use a cluster placement group with Capacity Reservations because a placement group influences network proximity, not tenancy or license visibility, and a Capacity Reservation only holds capacity — neither gives you a host, socket, or core identity.\n\nFor the exam, also know that Dedicated Hosts integrate with AWS License Manager, which tracks license consumption per host and can block instance launches that would exceed your entitlement — the standard answer when a stem adds 'and enforce that we never exceed our licensed core count'. Note also that Savings Plans do not apply to Dedicated Host usage; Dedicated Host Reservations are the discount mechanism there.",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "tags": [
      "EC2 Dedicated Hosts",
      "Dedicated Instances",
      "BYOL",
      "tenancy",
      "cost optimization"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon EC2 > User Guide > Dedicated Hosts",
      "AWS Documentation > AWS License Manager > User Guide > Host resource groups"
    ],
    "inShort": "Only Dedicated Hosts expose host, socket, and core detail, which socket/core-based BYOL licensing requires.",
    "trap": "Dedicated Instances also give dedicated hardware, but they expose no host or core visibility, so BYOL socket licensing fails."
