    "id": "d4-compute-cost-32",
    "domainId": "d4",
    "topicId": "d4-compute-cost",
    "type": "single",
    "stem": "You support a trading platform that must be able to launch 40 additional c6i.4xlarge instances in eu-west-1a within seconds on quarterly settlement days. Your team created 40 On-Demand Capacity Reservations for that instance type and AZ two months ago and has left them in place. Last month only 6 instances were ever running against the reservations, yet the bill for that instance type barely changed. Which statement BEST explains the charges, and what is the MOST cost-effective correction?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Capacity Reservations bill at the On-Demand rate for every reserved instance-hour whether or not instances run in them; cancel the reservations between settlement days and re-create them shortly before each event"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Capacity Reservations are free until instances occupy them; the charges must come from the EBS volumes attached to the six running instances"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Capacity Reservations bill only for unused capacity as a penalty; buy Regional Reserved Instances instead so the unused hours become free"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Capacity Reservations bill at 50% of the On-Demand rate when idle; convert them to a 1-year Compute Savings Plan to eliminate the idle charge"
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "An On-Demand Capacity Reservation holds capacity for a specific instance type, platform, and Availability Zone, and it is billed at the equivalent On-Demand rate for every hour it is active — whether an instance is sitting in it or not. Leaving 40 reservations open for two months to serve a handful of quarterly days means paying for 40 instances continuously. Because Capacity Reservations have no term commitment and can be created and cancelled at any time, the correct fix is to create them shortly before each settlement window and cancel them afterward.\n\nYou would not choose the answer that says reservations are free until occupied, because that is precisely the misconception being tested; EBS charges for six instances cannot account for the cost of 40 reserved instance slots.\n\nYou would not choose the 'penalty for unused capacity' framing with Regional RIs, because Regional Reserved Instances provide a billing discount and AZ flexibility but explicitly do NOT reserve capacity in a specific AZ — swapping to them would remove the capacity guarantee you bought the reservations for. (A zonal RI does reserve capacity, and it also bills continuously.)\n\nYou would not choose the 50% idle rate answer, because no such half-rate exists. A Savings Plan is only a discount mechanism applied on top of usage — it can reduce the rate charged for an active Capacity Reservation, but it never makes idle reserved hours free.\n\nFor the exam, also know EC2 Capacity Blocks for ML: for GPU-heavy training you pre-book accelerated capacity (for example P5 instances) for a defined future start date and duration, paid up front — the right answer when a stem needs guaranteed accelerated capacity for a scheduled run without a 1- or 3-year commitment.",
    "difficulty": 3,
    "tags": [
      "EC2 Capacity Reservations",
      "Capacity Blocks",
      "billing",
      "Reserved Instances",
      "cost optimization"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon EC2 > User Guide > On-Demand Capacity Reservations",
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon EC2 > User Guide > Capacity Blocks for ML"
    ],
    "inShort": "Capacity Reservations bill at On-Demand rates continuously, so cancel them when the capacity guarantee is not needed.",
    "trap": "Regional RIs look like the cheaper fix, but they give a discount without reserving AZ capacity — the guarantee disappears."
