    "id": "d4-cost-governance-18",
    "domainId": "d4",
    "topicId": "d4-cost-governance",
    "type": "single",
    "stem": "You are the solutions architect for an ad-tech company running about 600 resources in a single AWS account on Business Support. Finance has asked for a one-stop cleanup audit that identifies, in the same place: Classic Load Balancers that averaged fewer than 100 requests per day over the last 7 days, Elastic IP addresses not attached to any running instance, and EC2 instances averaging under 10% CPU. Which service will report ALL of these findings?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "AWS Trusted Advisor Cost Optimization checks"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "AWS Compute Optimizer"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "AWS Cost Explorer rightsizing recommendations"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "AWS Config with a conformance pack of managed rules"
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "AWS Trusted Advisor's Cost Optimization category is the only one of these that covers all three resource types in a single report. Its checks include idle load balancers (a load balancer averaging fewer than 100 requests per day over the last 7 days, or one with no active or no healthy back-end instances), unassociated Elastic IP addresses, and low-utilization Amazon EC2 instances (daily average CPU of 10% or less and network I/O of 5 MB or less on 4 or more of the previous 14 days), alongside underutilized EBS volumes, idle RDS instances, underutilized Redshift clusters, and Reserved Instance and Savings Plans optimization recommendations. The account is on Business Support, so the full cost check set is available.\n\nYou would not use AWS Compute Optimizer because its scope is rightsizing compute, storage, and database resources it has CloudWatch history for — EC2 instances, Auto Scaling groups, EBS volumes, Lambda functions, ECS services on Fargate, RDS DB instances, and commercial software licenses on EC2. It would catch the low-CPU instances and recommend a smaller family or size, but it has no concept of an Elastic IP address or a load balancer, so two of the three findings would be missing.\n\nYou would not use AWS Cost Explorer rightsizing recommendations because those are surfaced from Compute Optimizer data and are limited to EC2 instances — idle or over-provisioned instances only. Cost Explorer shows you where money went by service, account, or tag; it does not enumerate orphaned network addresses or request counts on a load balancer.\n\nYou would not use AWS Config because it evaluates configuration state and compliance, not utilization. A managed rule such as eip-attached would catch the unassociated Elastic IPs, but Config has no visibility into 14 days of CPU averages or load balancer request volume, so it cannot produce the idle-resource findings.\n\nFor the exam, also know that Trusted Advisor and Compute Optimizer are the classic confusable pair in cost questions. If the stem asks \"which specific instance type or size should I move to,\" that is Compute Optimizer (free, ML-driven, EC2/ASG/EBS/Lambda/Fargate/RDS). If the stem lists mixed waste such as idle load balancers, unattached Elastic IPs, or Reserved Instance optimization, that is Trusted Advisor.",
    "difficulty": 2,
