    "id": "d3-networking-perf-48",
    "domainId": "d3",
    "topicId": "d3-networking-perf",
    "type": "single",
    "stem": "You are the on-call architect for an insurance company. An EC2 instance in a private subnet cannot reach an Amazon RDS DB instance in another VPC. The application team has already rebooted everything. The two VPCs are connected through a transit gateway, and the path crosses several security groups, custom network ACLs, and multiple route tables. Your security policy forbids installing packet-capture tooling on production instances. You need to identify the exact component blocking the path with the LEAST operational effort. What should you do?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Run VPC Reachability Analyzer between the instance's elastic network interface and the network interface used by the RDS DB instance, and read the blocking component it reports"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Enable VPC Flow Logs on both subnets, generate test traffic, and correlate REJECT records in Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Enable AWS CloudTrail data events for Amazon EC2 and search for denied network API calls"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Create a Network Access Analyzer scope for internet access and review the findings"
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "VPC Reachability Analyzer is the purpose-built path validation tool. You choose a source and destination such as an ENI or a gateway, and it statically evaluates security groups, network ACLs, route tables, peering connections, and transit gateway route tables to determine whether the path is reachable. When it is not, it identifies the exact blocking component, so you go straight to the offending rule with no packet capture and no test traffic.\n\nYou would not start with VPC Flow Logs because they record only traffic that was actually attempted; you must generate load, wait for log delivery, and then infer which hop dropped the packet. Flow Logs are excellent for observed behavior but slower and less direct than a configuration analysis that names the component.\n\nYou would not use CloudTrail because it records AWS API activity such as who modified a security group, not packet-level connectivity between an instance and a database.\n\nYou would not use Network Access Analyzer here because it answers the inverse question — finding unintended paths that exist across your network against a defined access scope — rather than diagnosing why one specific intended path fails.\n\nFor the exam, know the pairing: Reachability Analyzer for \"could this traffic get through, and what is blocking it,\" Flow Logs for \"what traffic actually happened and was it accepted or rejected,\" and Network Access Analyzer for \"which resources can reach places our policy says they should not.\"",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "tags": [
      "Reachability Analyzer",
      "Network Access Analyzer",
      "VPC troubleshooting",
      "VPC Flow Logs"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon VPC > Reachability Analyzer > What is Reachability Analyzer?",
      "AWS Documentation > Amazon VPC > Network Access Analyzer > What is Network Access Analyzer?"
    ],
    "inShort": "Reachability Analyzer statically tests a path and names the exact security group, ACL, or route blocking it.",
    "trap": "Flow Logs are the classic reflex, but they need real traffic and only show REJECTs — they do not name the blocking configuration."
