    "id": "d4-network-cost-45",
    "domainId": "d4",
    "topicId": "d4-network-cost",
    "type": "single",
    "stem": "You are the network architect for a design-software company where roughly 120 remote engineers use AWS Client VPN to reach a single VPC in eu-west-1, but only during an 8-hour workday on weekdays. To make failover simple, a previous engineer associated the Client VPN endpoint with subnets in all three Availability Zones and left it in place permanently. Finance reports that the Client VPN line item is far higher than the connected-user count suggests. What is the MOST likely cause and the MOST cost-effective fix?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Client VPN bills an hourly charge per associated subnet regardless of whether anyone is connected; reduce the endpoint to a single subnet association and disassociate it outside business hours."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Client VPN bills a flat monthly endpoint fee per VPC; delete the endpoint and replace it with an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection for the engineers."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Client VPN charges are driven by data processing per GB; enable split-tunnel and move the endpoint into AWS Cloud WAN to lower the per-GB rate."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Client VPN bills per client certificate issued; rotate to a shorter certificate lifetime and re-issue certificates only for active engineers."
      }
    ],
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "AWS Client VPN has two meters: an hourly charge for each subnet associated with the endpoint, and an hourly charge for each connected client. Three subnet associations accrue three times the association charge every hour of every day, including nights and weekends when nobody is connected, which explains a bill that is out of proportion to user connections. Associating a single subnet and disassociating it outside the workday removes that idle spend. A single association is not a functional downgrade for this workload: clients still reach resources in the other Availability Zones through normal VPC routing.\n\nYou would not replace it with Site-to-Site VPN because Site-to-Site VPN connects a network to a VPC over a customer gateway device and is billed per VPN connection-hour plus data transfer out. It does not provide per-user client software access for 120 roaming engineers, and there is no flat monthly per-VPC endpoint fee for Client VPN.\n\nYou would not attribute the cost to per-GB data processing on Client VPN. Split-tunnel is a good practice for reducing data transfer out, but it does not touch association hours, and AWS Cloud WAN is a global network fabric billed per core network edge, per attachment, and per GB — adding it here would increase cost, not lower a per-GB rate.\n\nYou would not attribute the cost to certificates. Client VPN mutual authentication uses AWS Certificate Manager certificates, which are free for this use, and certificate count is not a billing dimension.\n\nFor the exam, also know that Transit Gateway route tables are free to create, so isolating production, development, and shared services into separate Transit Gateway route tables costs nothing extra — Transit Gateway charges remain per attachment-hour plus per GB processed.",
    "difficulty": 3,
    "tags": [
      "Client VPN",
      "cost optimization",
      "Site-to-Site VPN",
      "Cloud WAN",
      "idle resources"
    ],
    "references": [
      "AWS Documentation > AWS Client VPN > Administrator Guide > Client VPN endpoints",
      "AWS Documentation > AWS Client VPN > Administrator Guide > Target networks"
    ],
    "inShort": "Client VPN bills per associated subnet per hour even with zero users, plus per connected client hour.",
    "trap": "Site-to-Site VPN seems cheaper, but it connects networks via a customer gateway and cannot give 120 roaming users client access."
